 "source_type": "instagram",
 "source_url": "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DfLFl_SKghz/",
 "title": "Never End Your Presentation With Questions",
 "topic": "communication",
 "summary": "Ending a presentation with 'Any questions?' risks leaving the audience with an awkward or random final impression. Instead, use the 'Last Word Technique' to answer questions early and finish with a prepared closing statement.",
 "key_points": [
  "Never end a talk with 'Any questions?' because it risks ending on a random, inappropriate question or awkward silence.",
  "The creator claims to have given over 500 keynotes over the past decade for companies like Google, FedEx, and Sony.",
  "The peak-end theory states that people typically remember two things from an event: the emotional peak and the conclusion.",
  "The creator recommends the 'Last Word Technique' to control how a talk ends.",
  "Say 'We have time for a few questions and then I'll wrap up' near the end of your presentation.",
  "Answer the questions, then finish with a pre-written close, such as a final story or a memorable line.",
  "This technique allows you to bring the audience back to your main point before they leave, even if the Q&A session goes off track."
 ],
 "content": "Never end your presentation with \"Any questions?\" It's how most talks end, but you risk ending your carefully crafted talk with a question that is random or even inappropriate, or even worse, with silence when no one raises their hand. I've given more than 500 keynotes over the past decade for companies like Google and FedEx and Sony, and here is what to do. Most of us put the questions last because it feels like the natural way to wrap up a talk. Where else would they go? But there's an important psychological principle at play here called the peak-end theory. It says that people typically remember two things from any event, the highlight or emotional peak and the conclusion. That means how you close a talk is disproportionately critical if you want them to remember it positively. We all know the energy of the room deflates when someone asks a weird or a random question. So instead of ending your talk on questions, use what I call the Last Word Technique. Here's how it works. You still take the questions, but you don't end on them. Near the end, you say, \"We have time for a few questions and then I'll wrap up.\" You answer them and then you finish with a close that you wrote ahead of time. It could be one last story or a line like, \"If you remember one thing from today, make it this.\" The faster our response time, the more likely our customers stay with us. It works because you're the one who decides how your talk ends. Even if the questions go somewhere strange, you get to bring everyone back to your main point before they leave. So before your next presentation, try the Last Word Technique and write out the way that you'll end your talk. And follow me for more on becoming a better communicator. Text on screen: Never end your presentation with \"Any questions?\"",
 "status": "done",
